body:
Hi, I'm studying on this ROS2(rclcpp) issue https://github.com/ros2/rclcpp/issues/755. I found that this bug is not likely to be happened on the other DDS vendor(OpenSplice). So, I tried to reproduce this situation using FastRTPS's native API. The following codes are some modifications of FastRTPS example([HelloWorldExample](https://github.com/eProsima/Fast-RTPS/tree/master/examples/C%2B%2B/HelloWorldExample)).

First, I made `HelloWorldPublisher` to publish rapidly(100 microseconds).

```
void HelloWorldPublisher::runThread(uint32_t samples, uint32_t sleep)
{
    if (samples == 0)
    {
        while(!stop)
        {
            if(publish(false))
            {
                std::cout << "Message: "<<m_Hello.message()<< " with index: "<< m_Hello.index()<< " SENT"<<std::endl;
            }
            // eClock::my_sleep(sleep);
            usleep(100);
        }
    }
    else
    {
        for(uint32_t i = 0;i<samples;++i)
        {
            if(!publish())
                --i;
            else
            {
                std::cout << "Message: "<<m_Hello.message()<< " with index: "<< m_Hello.index()<< " SENT"<<std::endl;
            }
            //eClock::my_sleep(sleep);
            usleep(100);
        }
    }
}
```

Second, print out the number of received message and the index of the latest received message after `HelloWorldSubscriber::run()`.

```
void HelloWorldSubscriber::run()
{
    std::cout << "Subscriber running. Please press enter to stop the Subscriber" << std::endl;
    std::cin.ignore();
    std::cout << "Subscribed count : " << this->m_listener.n_samples <<std::endl;
    std::cout << "Subscribed latest index : "  << this->m_listener.m_Hello.index() << std::endl;
}
```

Also, I give enough QoS resources to both of them.

```
Rparam.topic.historyQos.depth = 5000;
Rparam.topic.resourceLimitsQos.max_samples = 10000;
Rparam.topic.resourceLimitsQos.allocated_samples = 5000;
```

Lastly, I run `HelloWorldPublisher` before `HelloWorldSubscriber`. 

Terminal 1
```
HelloWorldExample publisher 2000
```

Terminal 2

```
HelloWorldExample subscriber
```

Then, the output is following.

Terminal 1
```
...
Message: HelloWorld with index: 1998 SENT
Message: HelloWorld with index: 1999 SENT
Message: HelloWorld with index: 2000 SENT
```

Terminal 2
```
...
Message HelloWorld 254 RECEIVED
Message HelloWorld 255 RECEIVED
Message HelloWorld 256 RECEIVED
Subscriber unmatched
(Enter key pressed)
Subscribed count : 256
Subscribed latest index : 256
```

I guess the expected output should be `Subscribed latest index : 2000` although `Subscribed count < 2000`. Is this right?
